pthreads: every pthread_cond_signal wakes a thread that is still waiting

pthread_cond_signal sent its signal to the first waiter on the condition's
list and left it there. A waiter took itself off the list only after it
had woken and locked the mutex again. So a signal could go to a thread
that was no longer waiting, and wake nobody:

1. Two signals in a row. With two threads waiting, two calls of
   pthread_cond_signal before the first waiter had run both went to the
   first waiter. The second thread slept on with its work queued.

2. A waiter whose wait had ended. A timed wait that had run out (or a wait
   woken by a break) stayed first on the list until it got the mutex. A
   pthread_cond_signal in that time went to it. It returned ETIMEDOUT, and
   a caller that gives up on ETIMEDOUT never looked at the condition, while
   the next waiter slept on. A stall makes this the usual order: every
   timer request that fell due during the stall completes at once when the
   machine runs again.

Now pthread_cond_signal and pthread_cond_broadcast take each waiter they
wake off the list (and mark it), so the next signal goes to the next
waiter. A waiter takes itself off the list as soon as it wakes, before it
locks the mutex, unless a signal has already taken it off. A timed wait
that ran out and was signalled as well returns 0, not ETIMEDOUT, so its
caller looks at the condition.

A waiter that has been signalled does not touch the condition again:
pthread_cond_destroy may follow a broadcast at once (C++ teardown does),
and it now finds the list empty and wipes the condition. So every change
to the list is made under Forbid, and a woken waiter looks at its own
node under Forbid alone, without the condition's semaphore. For the same
reason a cancelled thread that had been signalled as well does not pass
the signal on, as before this patch.

Test: tests/pthreads/condsignal.c.

diff --git a/pthreads/pthread.c b/pthreads/pthread.c
index 3047664..4f4134c 100644
--- a/pthreads/pthread.c
+++ b/pthreads/pthread.c
@@ -88,6 +88,7 @@ typedef struct {
 	struct MinNode node;
 	struct Task *task;
 	ULONG sigmask;
+	BOOL signalled;	// taken off the list by pthread_cond_signal or _broadcast
 } CondWaiter;
 
 typedef struct {
@@ -1150,25 +1151,37 @@ static int _pthread_cond_timedwait(pthread_cond_t *cond, pthread_mutex_t *mutex,
 		SetSignal(SIGF_COND_FALLBACK, 0);
 	}
 	waiter.sigmask = 1 << signal;
+	waiter.signalled = FALSE;
 	sigs |= waiter.sigmask | cancelmask;
 
 	// add it to the end of the list
 	ObtainSemaphore(&cond->semaphore);
+	Forbid();
 	AddTail((struct List*) &cond->waiters, (struct Node*) &waiter);
+	Permit();
 	ReleaseSemaphore(&cond->semaphore);
 
 	// wait for the condition to be signalled or the timeout
 	mutex->incond++;
 	pthread_mutex_unlock(mutex);
 	sigs = Wait(sigs);
+
+	// Leave the list now, before the mutex: the mutex can take a while, and a
+	// waiter that woke for its timeout or a break and was still listed took the
+	// next pthread_cond_signal, which then woke nobody. pthread_cond_signal and
+	// _broadcast take the waiters they wake off the list themselves, so a
+	// waiter that is not signalled here is still on it.
+	// Every change to the list is made under Forbid, and this one under Forbid
+	// alone: a signalled waiter must not touch the condition again, because
+	// pthread_cond_destroy may follow a broadcast at once and wipe it.
+	Forbid();
+	if (!waiter.signalled)
+		Remove((struct Node*) &waiter);
+	Permit();
+
 	pthread_mutex_lock(mutex);
 	mutex->incond--;
 
-	// remove the node from the list
-	ObtainSemaphore(&cond->semaphore);
-	Remove((struct Node*) &waiter);
-	ReleaseSemaphore(&cond->semaphore);
-
 	if (signal != SIGB_COND_FALLBACK)
 		FreeSignal(signal);
 
@@ -1183,14 +1196,18 @@ static int _pthread_cond_timedwait(pthread_cond_t *cond, pthread_mutex_t *mutex,
 		if (timermp.mp_SigBit != SIGB_TIMER_FALLBACK)
 			FreeSignal(timermp.mp_SigBit);
 
-		// did we timeout?
-		if (sigs & timermask)
+		// did we timeout? Not if a signal reached this waiter as well: it was
+		// the one thread that signal woke, so the caller must look at its
+		// condition, which ETIMEDOUT lets it skip.
+		if ((sigs & timermask) && !waiter.signalled)
 			timedout = 1;
 	}
 
 	// woken by pthread_cancel: the mutex is held again and the waiter is gone, as
 	// POSIX has it for a cancelled wait. (A Ctrl-C with no cancel behind it is a
 	// spurious wakeup, which a caller of pthread_cond_wait already allows for.)
+	// (A signal that reached this waiter as well is not passed on: the condition
+	// may be gone by now.)
 	if (sigs & cancelmask)
 		pthread_testcancel();
 
@@ -1227,14 +1244,21 @@ static int _pthread_cond_broadcast(pthread_cond_t *cond, BOOL onlyfirst) {
 	if (SemaphoreIsInvalid(&cond->semaphore))
 		pthread_cond_init(cond, NULL);
 
-	// signal the waiting threads 
+	// signal the waiting threads. Each one woken is taken off the list here, so
+	// a second pthread_cond_signal before the first waiter has run wakes the
+	// next waiter, not the same one again. The waiter's node is on its stack:
+	// it stays valid because the waiter looks at it under Forbid before it
+	// returns, and Forbid is held here from the RemHead to the Signal.
 	ObtainSemaphore(&cond->semaphore);
-	ForeachNode(&cond->waiters, waiter)
+	Forbid();
+	while ((waiter = (CondWaiter*) RemHead((struct List*) &cond->waiters)) != NULL)
 	{
+		waiter->signalled = TRUE;
 		Signal(waiter->task, waiter->sigmask);
 		if (onlyfirst)
 			break;
 	}
+	Permit();
 	ReleaseSemaphore(&cond->semaphore);
 
 	return 0;
